Chapter: ProbeDeckSubstring
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Does a text put on the DECK actually go on the deck?

 This question only became askable once tier 3 measured substring. Bare metal
 always copies a substring, and the copy is bumped off `r10` -- the LIVE
 allocation register, which inside a deck extent is the deck cursor. So on
 bare metal, taking a substring between `__deck-enter` and `__deck-exit` puts
 the bytes on the deck, and they outlive a rewind of the frontier. That is the
 whole point of the deck.

 `cx_substring` allocates nothing. It returns a slice of its argument, so the
 bytes stay exactly where the argument's bytes were -- on the frontier, if
 that is where the source was built. A deck extent cannot move them, because
 there is no allocation for the extent to redirect. The value looks decked and
 is not.

 If that is right, the first assertion below says yes on bare metal and no
 here, and it is a live corruption in the emit phase rather than a curiosity:
 the accumulator tables `emit-all-defs` carries across brackets are `List
 Text`, and a text that was supposed to be copied onto the deck but is
 secretly a slice of reclaimed frontier is a table holding garbage.

 CONCAT is tested beside it because the reasoning predicts the opposite
 answer, and a prediction that only ever confirms is not worth making. Our
 in-place concat extends a block when the block ends exactly at `cx_hp` --
 and inside an extent `cx_hp` IS the deck cursor, so a frontier-resident left
 operand fails that test and falls back to a copy, which lands on the deck.
 Accidentally correct, for a reason worth writing down: the fast path is
 guarded on the LIVE cursor rather than on a remembered one.

 The rewind is made observable rather than assumed. After the restore the
 probe allocates over the reclaimed span, so a dangling slice reads as the
 clobber instead of quietly still reading correctly because nothing happened
 to touch it yet.

 Explicit `__deck-enter`/`__deck-exit` rather than `deck-record`, because bare
 metal only intercepts `deck-record` when its defining chapter matches
 `init-phase-allocator`'s and this chapter is neither -- finding 25. The
 decomposition is honoured by every arm.

 One deck reservation serves both sections, and it is set AT the base with the
 frontier lifted above it. The first draft set the mark at `base + 8388608`
 and then advanced the frontier by the same 8388608, which puts both cursors
 on the same address; `cx_frontier_crosses` said so and named both. Worth
 recording because the guard doing its job looks exactly like a finding until
 you read the numbers it prints.

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- a substring taken inside a deck extent, then the frontier rewound --"
   let base = __heap-save
   in let m = __deck-set base
   in let lift = __heap-advance 1048576
   in let h = __heap-save
   in let src = "abcdefgh" & "ijklmnop"
   in let d1 = __deck-enter
   in let kept = substring src 2 4
   in let d2 = __deck-exit
   in let back = __heap-restore h
   in let clobber = "ZZZZZZZZ" & "ZZZZZZZZ"
   in act
     print-line-uni ("length survived           : " & show-yn (text-length kept == 4))
     print-line-uni ("BYTES survived            : " & show-yn (text-compare kept "cdef" == 0))
     print-line-uni ("  (no = it was a slice of the frontier, not a copy on the deck)")
   end
   print-line-uni "-- the same shape with concat, which is predicted to survive --"
   let h2 = __heap-save
   in let a = "abcd" & "efgh"
   in let d3 = __deck-enter
   in let joined = a & "XY"
   in let d4 = __deck-exit
   in let back2 = __heap-restore h2
   in let clobber2 = "ZZZZZZZZ" & "ZZZZZZZZ"
   in act
     print-line-uni ("concat bytes survived     : " & show-yn (text-compare joined "abcdefghXY" == 0))
     print-line-uni ("  (yes = the in-place path is guarded on the live cursor)")
   end
  end
